(118, 'A patient s/p TURP has pink-tinged urine and bladder spasms. This is:', '["Normal", "Indicative of infection", "Sign of hemorrhage", "Urinary retention"]'::jsonb, 0),
(119, 'A patient with facial burns, singed nasal hair, and hoarseness of voice after a house fire should be closely monitored for:', '["No specific concern beyond the visible burns", "Inhalational airway injury, with high risk of rapid airway obstruction", "Simple anxiety", "Only skin infection risk"]'::jsonb, 1),
(120, 'To prevent pressure ulcers, a critically ill patient should be turned every:', '["30 minutes", "1 hour", "2 hours", "6 hours"]'::jsonb, 2),
(121, 'The general adaptation syndrome of stress includes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["An alarm reaction (fight-or-flight)", "A resistance phase", "A period of fast recovery", "A period of exhaustion"]'::jsonb, 2),
(122, 'A critically ill patient in septic shock remains hypotensive despite adequate fluid resuscitation and high-dose vasopressors. Which endocrine abnormality should be considered and evaluated?', '["Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency)", "Primary hyperaldosteronism", "Hyperthyroidism", "Growth hormone excess"]'::jsonb, 0),
(123, 'The following are indication of monitoring intra-abdominal pressure except:', '["Shock requiring resuscitation.", "Damage control laparotomy", "Major burns \\> 25%", "Laparotomy closed with normal tension"]'::jsonb, 3),
(124, 'Which of the following best describes the purpose of stress-ulcer prophylaxis (e.g. proton pump inhibitors) in critically ill patients?', '["To treat active pneumonia", "To reduce the risk of stress-related gastrointestinal bleeding in high-risk ICU patients", "To lower blood glucose", "To prevent deep vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 1),
(125, 'Hemorrhagic discoloration of the flanks is known as', '["Grey-Turner''s sign", "Grey-Prumer''s sign", "Cullen''s sign", "Kullen''s sign"]'::jsonb, 0),
(126, 'A hallmark early clinical clue suggesting necrotising fasciitis, distinguishing it from simple cellulitis, is:', '["Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings", "Mild, well-localised redness with minimal pain", "Slow onset over several weeks", "Complete absence of systemic symptoms"]'::jsonb, 0),
(127, 'The ''Golden Hour'' concept in trauma care refers to:', '["The first hour after hospital discharge", "The first 60 minutes after major trauma, during which rapid assessment and definitive care most improve survival", "The hour before surgery begins", "A 60-minute waiting period before treatment can start"]'::jsonb, 1),
(128, 'A common complication of severe hypothermia is:', '["Hyperactivity", "Increased body temperature", "Cardiac arrest (arrhythmias)", "Rapid breathing"]'::jsonb, 2),
(129, 'A properly functioning underwater-seal drain typically shows:', '["Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber", "No movement at all in the water-seal chamber", "Fluctuation (tidaling) and intermittent bubbling with respiration", "A water level that never moves"]'::jsonb, 2),
(130, 'A key element of the "ventilator bundle" (VAP prevention bundle) includes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Head-of-bed elevation 30-45 degrees", "Daily sedation vacation and readiness-to-wean assessment", "Peptic ulcer disease and deep vein thrombosis prophylaxis", "Routine daily scheduled ventilator circuit changes"]'::jsonb, 3),
(131, 'All are signs of ruptured ectopic pregnancy EXCEPT:', '["Tachycardia, shock, pallor and collapse", "Hyperglycaemia", "Abdominal pain", "Vaginal bleeding"]'::jsonb, 1),
(132, 'A pregnant patient at 32 weeks presents with painless, bright red vaginal bleeding. This presentation is most concerning for:', '["Placental abruption", "Placenta praevia", "Normal spotting in pregnancy", "Round ligament pain"]'::jsonb, 1),
(133, 'What are the symptoms of premature rupture of membranes?', '["Feeling a gush of fluid from the vagina", "Slow leak or trickle of fluid", "The fluid is clear and odourless", "All of the options"]'::jsonb, 3),
(134, 'A cervical incompetence diagnosis in a pregnant patient primarily increases the risk of:', '["Post-term pregnancy", "Second-trimester pregnancy loss or preterm birth from painless cervical dilation", "Gestational diabetes", "Multiple pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 1),
(135, 'A Rhesus-negative mother delivers a Rhesus-positive baby. The nurse anticipates administration of:', '["No specific treatment is needed", "Anti-D immunoglobulin to the mother to prevent sensitisation in future pregnancies", "Anti-D immunoglobulin to the baby", "Oxytocin to the mother for this purpose"]'::jsonb, 1),
(136, 'The most common cause of postpartum haemorrhage, among the 4 Ts, is:', '["Tone (uterine atony)", "Trauma", "Tissue", "Thrombin"]'::jsonb, 0),
(137, 'What important modification should be made when performing CPR on a pregnant patient in later pregnancy?', '["Manual left uterine displacement to relieve aortocaval compression and improve venous return", "Place the patient in the prone position", "Avoid performing chest compressions entirely", "Compress the abdomen instead of the chest"]'::jsonb, 0),
(138, 'Tracheo-oesophageal fistula is often associated with which other congenital anomaly?', '["Oesophageal atresia", "Cleft lip only", "Club foot only", "Polydactyly only"]'::jsonb, 0),
(139, 'Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency results in which potential maternal complication of hyperemesis gravidarum?', '["Renal failure", "Esophageal rupture", "Wernicke encephalopathy", "Osmotic demyelination syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2),
(140, 'A nurse is performing neonatal resuscitation. The newborn''s heart rate is below 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation. The next step is to:', '["Continue positive-pressure ventilation for another 30 seconds", "Administer epinephrine", "Initiate chest compressions", "Stop resuscitation and call the physician"]'::jsonb, 2),
(141, 'The most widely used effective treatment for neonatal jaundice is:', '["Blood transfusion", "Antibiotics", "Phototherapy", "Liver transplant"]'::jsonb, 2),
(142, 'Pyloric stenosis in infants classically presents with:', '["Bilious vomiting from birth", "Projectile, non-bilious vomiting typically starting around 3–6 weeks of age", "Chronic diarrhoea", "Bloody stools with fever"]'::jsonb, 1),
(143, 'Intussusception in infants classically presents with:', '["Painless abdominal distension only", "Episodic colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and ''redcurrant jelly'' stools", "Chronic constipation with no acute symptoms", "A silent, asymptomatic abdominal mass"]'::jsonb, 1),
(144, 'Regarding the newborn which of this is correct?', '["Preterms are babies delivered before 40 weeks of delivery", "All preterms are low birth weight", "All preterms are small for gestational age", "The term newborn may be small for gestational age"]'::jsonb, 3),
(145, 'Which is part of pre-operative care for a child with Hirschsprung''s disease?', '["CT scan", "Shaving the axillary hair", "Giving bowel washouts/enemas", "Obtaining consent from the child himself"]'::jsonb, 2),
(146, 'Fluid therapy in a severely dehydrated child typically begins with:', '["Oral fluids only, regardless of severity", "A rapid IV fluid bolus for severe dehydration, followed by ongoing maintenance and deficit replacement", "Withholding all fluids until specialist review", "Only subcutaneous fluids"]'::jsonb, 1),
(147, 'A characteristic sign of croup is:', '["A barking cough with inspiratory stridor", "A productive cough with green sputum", "Expiratory wheeze only", "High fever with drooling"]'::jsonb, 0),
(148, 'Febrile convulsions in young children are most commonly associated with:', '["A rapid rise in body temperature, often with a viral infection", "Chronic malnutrition alone", "Congenital heart disease exclusively", "Vitamin overdose"]'::jsonb, 0),
(149, 'A sickle cell disease patient presented with chest pain, low Spo2, cough, dyspnea, which of these is the cause?', '["Acute Asthmatic", "Acute Chest Syndrome", "Vaso-Occlusive Crisis", "Acute Anaemia"]'::jsonb, 1),
(150, 'Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS) risk is reduced by which key recommendation?', '["Placing infants to sleep prone (on their stomach)", "Placing infants to sleep supine (on their back)", "Co-sleeping in the same bed as an adult at all times", "Using soft, loose bedding in the crib"]'::jsonb, 1),
(151, 'Florence Nightingale''s Environmental Theory, published in ''Notes on Nursing,'' primarily focuses on which idea?', '["Nursing should focus on manipulating the client''s environment to place the body in the best condition for nature to act and for healing to occur", "Nursing should focus exclusively on administering medications prescribed by physicians", "Illness is caused solely by genetic predisposition and cannot be influenced by environment", "The nurse''s primary role is to perform invasive procedures independent of physicians"]'::jsonb, 0),
(152, 'Which of the following is one of Henderson''s 14 basic needs related to physiological function?', '["Breathe normally", "Achieve self-actualization", "Maintain the flexible line of defense", "Engage in role-function adaptation"]'::jsonb, 0),
(153, 'Orem identified three types of nursing systems. One of them is:', '["Team nursing", "Primary nursing", "Individualised client care", "Wholly compensatory"]'::jsonb, 3),
(154, 'In Sister Callista Roy''s Adaptation Model, the person is viewed as:', '["A biopsychosocial adaptive system that receives input (stimuli) and produces output in the form of adaptive or ineffective responses", "A passive recipient of environmental changes with no internal processing mechanisms", "A system defined solely by 14 basic needs", "An energy field indistinguishable from the environment"]'::jsonb, 0),
(155, 'The following Stressors that disrupt the system according Neumann''s model include ------ ------', '["The primary, secondary, and tertiary stressor.", "The individuals, families, and groups stressor", "Physiological, sociocultural, and developmental stressor", "Intra-personal, inter- personal, and extra personal stressors."]'::jsonb, 3),
(156, 'A nurse using Rogers'' Science of Unitary Human Beings as a guiding framework would most likely incorporate which of the following into practice?', '["Therapeutic touch or other modalities intended to promote harmonious patterning of the human and environmental energy fields", "Strict enforcement of the 14 basic needs checklist", "Ranking patient needs using Maslow''s pyramid exclusively", "Assessment limited only to the 11 functional health patterns"]'::jsonb, 0),
(157, 'Marjory Gordon''s Functional Health Patterns framework is primarily used by nurses to:', '["Diagnose medical conditions independently of physicians", "Organize a systematic, holistic nursing assessment across defined categories of client behavior over time", "Replace the nursing process entirely with a single-step evaluation", "Calculate a client''s position on the health-illness continuum numerically"]'::jsonb, 1),
(158, 'According to Maslow''s hierarchy, the most basic needs are:', '["Safety and security needs", "Self-actualisation needs", "Physiological needs", "Social needs"]'::jsonb, 2),
(159, 'The standard format for writing a ''problem-focused'' (actual) NANDA-I nursing diagnosis is often referred to as the PES format, meaning:', '["Problem, Etiology, Signs and symptoms (defining characteristics)", "Plan, Evaluate, Solve", "Patient, Environment, Standard", "Priority, Evidence, Solution"]'::jsonb, 0),
(160, 'Standardised nursing terminologies such as NANDA, NIC, and NOC exist to:', '["Make documentation more variable between institutions", "Provide a consistent language for nursing diagnoses, interventions, and outcomes", "Replace physician orders", "Track hospital finances"]'::jsonb, 1),
(161, 'Which of the following goal statements is correctly written in SMART format?', '["\"Client will feel better soon\"", "\"Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 meters) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift\"", "\"Nurse will help the client walk\"", "\"Client''s pain will improve\""]'::jsonb, 1),
(162, 'A retrospective nursing audit involves reviewing records:', '["During the care of the patient", "After the patient has been discharged", "Before the patient is admitted to the hospital", "To plan the patient''s care before it begins"]'::jsonb, 1),
(163, 'A ''null hypothesis'' (H0) states that', '["There is no statistically significant relationship or difference between the variables being studied", "There is definitely a significant relationship between the variables", "The researcher''s prediction is automatically correct", "Data collection is unnecessary"]'::jsonb, 0),
(164, 'Which variable is NOT manipulated by the researcher but measured as the outcome?', '["Extraneous variable", "Independent variable", "Dependent variable", "Internal variable"]'::jsonb, 2),
(165, 'Probability sampling gives every member of the population:', '["Equal representation", "Guaranteed selection", "An equal chance of being selected", "No chance of selection"]'::jsonb, 2),
(166, 'A patient''s body weight measured in kilograms is an example of which scale of measurement?', '["Nominal", "Ordinal", "Interval", "Ratio"]'::jsonb, 3),
(167, 'The following are Sources of literature review EXCEPT', '["dissertations", "existing theory", "Internet", "Social medias"]'::jsonb, 3),
(168, 'The "mean" of a data set is:', '["The most frequently occurring value", "The middle value when data are arranged in order", "The sum of all values divided by the number of values", "The difference between the highest and lowest values"]'::jsonb, 2),
(169, 'In most nursing and health research, a result is typically considered "statistically significant" when the p-value is:', '["Greater than 0.05", "Less than or equal to 0.05", "Equal to 1.0", "Greater than 0.5"]'::jsonb, 1),
(170, 'The Chi-square test is most appropriately used to analyse:', '["The relationship or association between two categorical (nominal) variables", "The correlation between two continuous variables", "The mean difference between two groups", "The standard deviation of a single sample"]'::jsonb, 0),
(171, 'A quasi-experimental design differs from a true experimental design mainly because it', '["Lacks random assignment of subjects to groups, though it may still involve manipulation of the independent variable", "Never involves any form of intervention", "Always has a larger sample size than true experiments", "Cannot be used to study cause-and-effect relationships at all"]'::jsonb, 0),
(172, 'A pilot study provides the opportunity to:', '["Publish the results early", "Test the research subjects", "Understand the research problem", "Test the research instrument"]'::jsonb, 3),
(173, '''Reliability'' of a research instrument refers to', '["The consistency and stability with which the instrument produces the same results on repeated administration", "The degree to which the instrument measures the intended concept accurately", "The cost of producing and distributing the instrument", "The number of items contained in a questionnaire"]'::jsonb, 0),
(174, 'The first and most important function of a manager is:', '["Delegation", "Funding", "Planning", "Supervision"]'::jsonb, 2),
(175, 'Henri Fayol''s 14 principles include the following EXCEPT:', '["Division of labour", "Discipline", "Unity of command", "Strategic management"]'::jsonb, 3),
(176, 'The ‘father’ of Scientific Management is:', '["Henry Gantt", "W. Edwards Deming", "Henry Ford", "Frederick W. Taylor"]'::jsonb, 3),
(177, 'In Herzberg''s theory, the following are motivators EXCEPT:', '["Recognition", "Achievement", "Money (salary)", "Challenging work"]'::jsonb, 2),
(178, 'Which leadership style is most appropriate during a mass casualty emergency?', '["Democratic", "Laissez-faire", "Autocratic", "Affiliative"]'::jsonb, 2),
(179, 'A nurse delegates the administration of intravenous medication to a nursing assistant. This action is:', '["Appropriate", "Acceptable if supervised", "Inappropriate because it exceeds the assistant''s scope of practice", "Recommended during emergencies"]'::jsonb, 2),
(180, 'Which of this budget is not part types of budget?', '["Personnel budget", "Operational budget", "Capital budget", "Supplementary budget"]'::jsonb, 3),
(181, 'According to adult learning principles (andragogy), adults learn best when the content is:', '["Abstract, theoretical, and disconnected from real experience", "Relevant, problem-centred, and builds on their prior experience", "Delivered purely through passive lecture with no interaction", "Identical regardless of the learner''s background"]'::jsonb, 1),
(182, 'A method of Teaching includes all the following Except....', '["Explanation", "Lecturing", "Demonstration", "Tertiary"]'::jsonb, 3),
(183, 'Evaluation that occurs continuously throughout the implementation of care (e.g., during each shift) is called:', '["Summative evaluation", "Formative (ongoing) evaluation", "Structure evaluation", "Terminal evaluation"]'::jsonb, 1),
(184, 'An incident report should be completed when:', '["A medication error occurs", "A patient is discharged", "A nurse resumes duty", "The ward is cleaned"]'::jsonb, 0),
(185, 'The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN), as a professional regulatory body, is primarily responsible for:', '["Setting nursing education standards, registering/licensing nurses, and enforcing professional discipline", "Only organising nursing conferences", "Managing hospital construction budgets", "Setting national drug prices"]'::jsonb, 0),
(186, 'The doctrine of ''Res Ipsa Loquitur'' (''the thing speaks for itself'') applies when:', '["A patient explicitly consents to a risky procedure", "An injury is of a type that would not normally occur without someone''s negligence (e.g. a retained surgical instrument)", "A nurse documents care accurately", "A patient refuses treatment"]'::jsonb, 1),
(187, 'The four elements that must be proven to establish nursing negligence are:', '["Consent, capacity, competence, and cost", "Duty, breach of duty, causation, and damages", "Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, and justice", "Assessment, diagnosis, planning, and evaluation"]'::jsonb, 1),
(188, '''Battery'' in a legal/nursing context refers to:', '["A device that powers medical equipment", "Unlawful physical contact with a patient without consent", "A type of medication error", "A category of documentation"]'::jsonb, 1),
(189, 'For informed consent to be legally valid, it must include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Adequate information given to the patient", "Patient capacity to understand the information", "Voluntary agreement, free from coercion", "Approval from a family member instead of the patient, when the patient has full capacity"]'::jsonb, 3),
(190, '''Veracity'' as an ethical principle in nursing means:', '["Loyalty and faithfulness to commitments", "Truthfulness and honesty with patients", "Fairness in resource distribution", "Avoiding harm"]'::jsonb, 1),
(191, 'A Do-Not-Resuscitate (DNR) order should be:', '["Assumed automatically for all elderly patients", "Clearly documented in the medical record and communicated to the entire care team", "Kept verbal only, never written", "Decided solely by the nurse without physician or patient/family involvement"]'::jsonb, 1),
(192, 'The Good Samaritan act covers:', '["Residents of long-term care facilities", "Passerby in car accidents", "Protection from civil liability when emergency care is provided in good faith", "Nurses who choose not to provide care in an emergency"]'::jsonb, 2),
(193, 'If you make a mistake in a record how should it be corrected?', '["With correction fluid", "Scribble it out", "Put a single line through the record", "Tell your mentor"]'::jsonb, 2),
(194, 'A conscious, competent adult patient refuses a blood transfusion on religious grounds despite life-threatening anaemia. The nurse''s ethically and legally appropriate response is to:', '["Administer the transfusion anyway, since the situation is life-threatening", "Respect the patient''s autonomous refusal after ensuring they are fully informed, and document thoroughly", "Have a family member override the patient''s decision", "Discharge the patient immediately with no further care"]'::jsonb, 1),
(195, 'Which of the following is a recognised advantage of the Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) over purely laboratory-based nutritional assessment?', '["It requires no clinician training", "It does not require any patient history", "It reliably predicts nutrition-related clinical outcomes and is not confounded by fluid shifts or acute-phase inflammatory changes the way serum albumin can be", "It replaces the need for anthropometric data entirely"]'::jsonb, 2),
(196, 'A patient on TPN is at risk for refeeding syndrome. The nurse should be most vigilant for which of the following electrolyte imbalances?', '["Hypernatremia", "Hyperkalemia", "Hypophosphatemia", "Hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 2),
(197, 'Enteral nutrition is generally preferred over parenteral nutrition when:', '["The gut is functioning", "The gut has completely failed", "Never — parenteral is always preferred", "Only in paediatric patients"]'::jsonb, 0),
(198, 'A nasogastric tube''s position should be confirmed before feeding by:', '["Auscultation (''whoosh test'') alone", "pH testing of aspirate and/or X-ray confirmation", "The patient''s verbal comfort report only", "Tube colour"]'::jsonb, 1),
(199, 'Manual hyperinflation', '["Involve the use of expiratory pause of 5-10 seconds", "Is used to provide fast and slow expiratory breath", "Technique is not used in the management of patient''s self-ventilating", "Technique is used in the management of ventilated patients"]'::jsonb, 3),
(200, 'Electrotherapy equipment includes:', '["MRI", "Mammogram", "Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)", "Spirometer"]'::jsonb, 2);
